WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.999 --> 00:00:04.659
Hello everyone. Our work is Capturing Rich
Details of Human

2
00:00:04.659 --> 00:00:08.959
Eyes. We reconstruct personalized eye
assets with detailed

3
00:00:09.239 --> 00:00:11.379
geometry, appearance, and pupil-driven
iris motion.

4
00:00:13.325 --> 00:00:16.425
Recovering these details requires
different kinds of evidence.

5
00:00:17.045 --> 00:00:20.045
The sclera has visible vessels and a
non-spherical shape. The

6
00:00:20.605 --> 00:00:23.905
transparent cornea reflects light and
refracts our view of the

7
00:00:23.905 --> 00:00:27.765
iris. Beneath it, iris pigmentation and
surface relief jointly

8
00:00:27.765 --> 00:00:31.325
determine appearance. A single capture
modality cannot readily

9
00:00:31.325 --> 00:00:32.385
separate these effects.

10
00:00:34.587 --> 00:00:37.867
We combine complementary optical
measurements with staged

11
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:42.047
reconstruction. Each branch targets a
different component. The

12
00:00:42.047 --> 00:00:45.687
sclera establishes the exterior reference;
the cornea supplies

13
00:00:45.687 --> 00:00:49.427
the refractive interface. Together, they
support recovery of the

14
00:00:49.427 --> 00:00:50.727
iris beneath that interface.

15
00:00:52.357 --> 00:00:56.317
A single gaze leaves much of the sclera
unobserved. We therefore

16
00:00:56.317 --> 00:00:59.597
combine multiple views and gaze directions
to improve surface

17
00:00:59.597 --> 00:01:03.717
coverage. Each gaze is reconstructed
separately, aligned in a

18
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:07.197
common spherical reference, and merged.
The result preserves

19
00:01:07.197 --> 00:01:10.157
scleral shape and vessels, while providing
a reference for the

20
00:01:10.157 --> 00:01:10.797
other components.

21
00:01:12.369 --> 00:01:15.869
The transparent cornea requires different
geometric evidence.

22
00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.769
Placido-ring reflections constrain surface
orientation,

23
00:01:18.769 --> 00:01:22.749
complemented by specular highlights. We
combine these

24
00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:26.449
measurements to recover the anterior
corneal surface. Its

25
00:01:26.449 --> 00:01:29.609
geometry determines how light bends
between the camera, the

26
00:01:29.609 --> 00:01:31.109
illumination, and the iris.

27
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:36.593
Refraction matters when recovering iris
detail. We account for

28
00:01:36.593 --> 00:01:39.693
it along viewing and illumination paths.
Multi-light images

29
00:01:39.693 --> 00:01:43.813
provide shading changes that constrain
surface normals. These

30
00:01:43.813 --> 00:01:47.513
normals guide geometric refinement,
allowing us to recover iris

31
00:01:47.513 --> 00:01:48.993
relief together with its texture.

32
00:01:50.561 --> 00:01:53.941
The recovered components form a complete,
renderable eye.

33
00:01:54.581 --> 00:01:57.741
Keeping their geometry explicit allows us
to model reflection

34
00:01:57.741 --> 00:02:01.061
and refraction while retaining access to
each surface for

35
00:02:01.061 --> 00:02:05.041
analysis. We apply the same workflow
across subjects, preserving

36
00:02:05.041 --> 00:02:09.101
their individual shapes, vessel patterns,
and iris textures. The

37
00:02:09.101 --> 00:02:12.541
resulting assets share a common
representation without replacing

38
00:02:12.541 --> 00:02:14.881
those captured differences with a single
eye shape.

39
00:02:16.483 --> 00:02:19.563
We also characterize individual
differences in geometry.

40
00:02:20.243 --> 00:02:23.403
Pupil-center offset describes alignment;
scleral topography

41
00:02:23.403 --> 00:02:27.703
measures deviations from a sphere; corneal
elevation describes

42
00:02:27.703 --> 00:02:31.463
local shape. These properties support
analysis of the recovered

43
00:02:31.463 --> 00:02:33.303
eye structure alongside its appearance.

44
00:02:34.999 --> 00:02:38.159
Across subjects, these measurements reveal
differences in

45
00:02:38.159 --> 00:02:42.139
surface shape and pupil-center offset. Our
personalized assets

46
00:02:42.139 --> 00:02:45.419
therefore retain geometric characteristics
as well as the colors

47
00:02:45.419 --> 00:02:47.159
and patterns captured in their textures.

48
00:02:48.937 --> 00:02:53.037
A static iris cannot represent changes in
pupil size. We

49
00:02:53.037 --> 00:02:56.457
therefore model deformation across pupil
states, keeping the

50
00:02:56.457 --> 00:02:59.377
outer boundary fixed as the opening
expands and contracts.

51
00:03:00.137 --> 00:03:03.217
Texture follows the surface, preserving
the captured pattern.

52
00:03:03.837 --> 00:03:07.317
The deformation also changes the surface
profile, allowing the

53
00:03:07.317 --> 00:03:10.397
model to express both the pupil opening
and the iris shape

54
00:03:10.397 --> 00:03:11.217
throughout the motion.

55
00:03:13.641 --> 00:03:17.461
This representation retains individual
pigmentation, radial

56
00:03:17.461 --> 00:03:21.301
patterns, and relief as the pupil changes.
The cornea adds

57
00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:24.821
reflection and refracts the iris beneath
it. These examples

58
00:03:24.821 --> 00:03:28.021
illustrate the resulting appearance. To
evaluate reconstruction

59
00:03:28.021 --> 00:03:31.841
quality, we need quantitative comparisons
of surface accuracy

60
00:03:31.841 --> 00:03:34.761
and image agreement, including the
contribution of each

61
00:03:34.761 --> 00:03:35.561
additional measurement.

62
00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:41.219
These comparisons test whether corneal
ring measurements and

63
00:03:41.219 --> 00:03:44.399
iris photometric normals improve the
recovered geometry.

64
00:03:46.011 --> 00:03:49.531
Synthetic observations provide known
reference geometry,

65
00:03:49.771 --> 00:03:52.271
allowing us to measure reconstruction
errors directly.

66
00:03:53.815 --> 00:03:57.415
In this synthetic evaluation, combining
ring and specular

67
00:03:57.415 --> 00:04:01.035
constraints improves corneal
reconstruction. For the iris,

68
00:04:01.255 --> 00:04:04.395
photometric normals reduce the mean
absolute error by half.

69
00:04:05.633 --> 00:04:08.893
To assess appearance, we compare
renderings with captured

70
00:04:08.893 --> 00:04:12.793
images. Reserving gaze directions allows
evaluation beyond

71
00:04:12.793 --> 00:04:16.513
reconstruction inputs. We also examine the
effects of iris

72
00:04:16.513 --> 00:04:18.713
photometric normals and corneal
refraction.

73
00:04:19.865 --> 00:04:23.505
Across twenty eyes, geometric and image
measurements provide

74
00:04:23.505 --> 00:04:26.285
complementary assessments of the
reconstructed assets.
